-- GAME ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  Title       Bomb Jack
  Publisher   Elite, 1986
  Version     1.1

-- YOU NEED ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Your own copy of the original game, saved beside this patch:

  File        Bomb Jack (1986)(Elite Systems).z80
  MD5         08e760e82721540898b57b8a1f277659

  The patch carries none of the game's code. It only works with that exact
  dump, under that name or any other: anything else is refused, with a
  message naming the file it wants.

-- WHAT IT DOES --------------------------------------------------------------
  Moving figures become Next hardware sprites, so they keep their own colours
  and no longer take on the scenery's (colour clash). Game logic is unchanged.

  Colours     Default (the game's own) plus: Enhanced, chosen on the menu, off
              by default.
  Enhanced    Figures in the arcade's colours: Jack blue and red with a white
              face, white mummies, grey birds with red, red bombs with a
              yellow glint, orange to yellow platforms. Only sprite colours
              change. A painted set, colourist not named.

  Changes     1.1: Enhanced colours, after the arcade, as a menu choice.

  Note        Jack, the enemies, the balls, coins and stars are hardware
              sprites, and the bombs and platforms are drawn over their ULA
              copies, so a figure no longer takes the colours of the
              background it crosses. The scenery and the scores stay as the
              game draws them. Sinclair's whole ROM under the game, so it
              starts from its BASIC line on NextZXOS. Proven on sixteen
              recordings.

-- MENU ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   1  Infinite lives
   2  Immunity
   3  No enemies

  From an archive .pok, each checked against this release's code: the lives
  DEC, the collision test's return and the enemy routine's entry. The two
  lives-count lines ask for a value and are left out.

